    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1890</span><span class="rf-text">Born Nguyễn Sinh Cung in Kim Liên commune, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nghe-an-province">Nghệ An province</a>, in Annam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1911</span><span class="rf-text">Sails from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> as a kitchen helper on the French steamer Amiral de Latouche-Tréville</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920</span><span class="rf-text">Votes for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Third International</a> at the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and becomes a founding member of the French Communist Party</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text">Chairs the meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> that unites two Vietnamese communist parties and founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">Returns to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> to lead the Việt Minh independence movement against the Japanese occupation</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a>, reads the Declaration of Independence of the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1954</span><span class="rf-text">The Việt Minh defeat the French at Điện Biên Phủ; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Accords divide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> at the 17th parallel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1969</span><span class="rf-text">Dies of heart failure at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, with the outcome of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> still in question</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The details of Hồ Chí Minh's early life are uncertain: he used between 50 and 200 pseudonyms, and at least four official biographies differ on names, dates and places. He was born Nguyễn Sinh Cung in 1890 in the village of Hoàng Trù in Kim Liên commune, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nghe-an-province">Nghệ An province</a>, then under French protection; 1890 is generally accepted, though at various times he gave four other years. His father, Nguyễn Sinh Sắc, was a Confucian scholar and teacher who later became an imperial magistrate and was demoted for abuse of power after a local figure died following a caning; he was both a patriot and a collaborator with the French colonial state. Following Confucian tradition, his father renamed him Nguyễn Tất Thành at the age of ten.</p><p>He mastered chữ Hán, the prerequisite for any serious study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, and then received a French education at the Collège Quốc học in Huế, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pham-van-dong">Phạm Văn Đồng</a>, Võ Nguyên Giáp and his later rival Ngô Đình Diệm also attended. A tradition that he took part in an anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> demonstration of 1908 is contradicted by a French archival document showing that he was admitted to the school months after it. In 1911, using the alias Văn Ba, he signed on as a kitchen helper on the French steamer Amiral de Latouche-Tréville and sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> to Marseille. Refused by the French Colonial School, he spent the years to 1917 working on ships; by his own accounts he lived for a time in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, though the evidence for much of it is thin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party.jpg" alt="Indochinese Communist Party" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_variant_of_Communist_Party_of_Vietnam_flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nghia7554, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1919 Nguyễn Tất Thành took up politics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He joined the Groupe des Patriotes Annamites, which petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> peace talks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> among others, for the civil rights of the Vietnamese and an end to French rule. It was ignored, but as the public face of the petition, which Phan Văn Trường had written, he became known as Nguyễn Ái Quốc ("Nguyễn the Patriot"). Many authors have called 1919 a lost "Wilsonian moment", but he was already committed to a socialist program. In December 1920 he voted for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Third International</a> at the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and became a founding member of the French Communist Party.</p><p>In 1923 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, worked for the Comintern and studied at the Communist University of the Toilers of the East. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>, he organised the Vietnamese Revolutionary Youth League and trained young Vietnamese revolutionaries. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s anti-communist coup of 1927 he left Canton, and by way of Moscow, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, where early in 1930 he chaired the meeting that united two Vietnamese communist parties and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a>. Arrested in Hong Kong in 1931, he was defended by the solicitor Frank Loseby and eventually released. In Moscow he was removed from control of the party he had founded, whose new leaders criticised his nationalist tendencies, but he returned to favour in 1937. In 1940 he and his lieutenants Võ Nguyên Giáp and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pham-van-dong">Phạm Văn Đồng</a> founded the League for Vietnamese Independence, the Việt Minh, in Guilin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/august-revolution.jpg" alt="August Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%A1ch_m%E1%BA%A1ng_th%C3%A1ng_8_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vũ Năng An, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hồ returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1941 to lead the Việt Minh against the Japanese occupation. Jailed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>'s local authorities and released in 1943, he began regularly using the name Hồ Chí Minh. In April 1945 he offered intelligence to the American Office of Strategic Services, which sent a team to train his men. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-revolution">August Revolution</a> and the abdication of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>, he read the Declaration of Independence of the Democratic Republic of Vietnam on 2 September 1945. No country recognised his government, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a> never answered his petitions. In March 1946, with 200,000 Chinese troops in the north, he signed an agreement recognising Vietnam as an autonomous state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, but it soon broke down. After the French Navy's bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiphong">Haiphong</a>, which reportedly killed more than 6,000 civilians, he declared war on 19 December 1946, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>.</p><p>His forces withdrew to the countryside and fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war. In February 1950 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and they agreed that China would back the Việt Minh. In 1954 more than 10,000 French soldiers surrendered at Điện Biên Phủ, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Accords divided Vietnam at the 17th parallel, with elections for reunification to be held in July 1956. Hồ's government became that of North Vietnam, a Communist-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, which he served as prime minister until 1955 and as president until his death. He also wrote poetry, among it Poems from the Prison Diary.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/phan-boi-chau"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phan-boi-chau.jpg" alt="Phan Bội Châu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/phan-boi-chau">Phan Bội Châu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phan_Boi_Chau.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1946, while Hồ was abroad, his subordinates imprisoned 2,500 non-communist nationalists and forced 6,000 others to flee; hundreds of political opponents were executed or exiled after a failed coup attempt that July, and the Việt Minh collaborated with French forces to massacre supporters of rival nationalist movements and the Trotskyists. According to Daniel Guérin, Hồ called the Trotskyist leader Tạ Thu Thâu "a great patriot" and then added: "All those who do not follow the line which I have laid down will be broken." Arthur Dommen estimates that the Việt Minh killed between 100,000 and 150,000 civilians during the war; Benjamin Valentino estimates that the French were responsible for 60,000–250,000 civilian deaths.</p><p>The land reform of 1953–1956 was accompanied by executions of "reactionary and evil landlords". Estimates at the time ran to 50,000 or more; declassified Vietnamese and Hungarian documents point to far fewer, though likely more than 13,500, while Gareth Porter estimated 800–2,500 and Balázs Szalontai found 1,337 executions by December 1955, before a harsher third stage. In 1956 Hồ apologised for the serious errors of the reform, and as many as 23,748 political prisoners were released by September 1957.</p><p>The anti-communist writer Hoàng Văn Chí argued that Hồ betrayed the nationalist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phan-boi-chau">Phan Bội Châu</a> to the French in 1925 for money; William Duiker rejected the charge, and Sophie Quinn-Judge and Duncan McCargo think it likely propaganda. The government remains sensitive to accounts of Hồ's private life and sought cuts in the translation of Duiker's biography.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/le-duan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/le-duan.jpg" alt="Lê Duẩn" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Lê Duẩn</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_duan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1959 Hồ urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> to aid the Việt Cộng in the South, and a "people's war" was approved. North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> built supply routes through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> that became known as the Hồ Chí Minh trail, and in December 1960 created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-algeria">National Liberation Front</a> of South Vietnam. Hồ informally chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Lê Duẩn</a> as the next party leader and in September 1960 stepped down as General Secretary, keeping a secondary role as head of state but considerable influence. In 1963 he showed interest in a French plan for a neutral federation of the two Vietnams, which faded after Diệm was overthrown and killed that November.</p><p>When American combat troops arrived and the bombing of the North began in 1965, Hồ insisted that there could be no peace talks unless the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> unconditionally stopped bombing, which he saw as a violation of North Vietnam's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. He made a secret visit to Mao in April 1965, and in 1967 agreed to the plan that became the Tet Offensive, wanting to see Vietnam reunified within his lifetime. In early 1969 he suffered a heart attack, and he answered a letter from President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> without concessions. He died of heart failure at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> on 2 September 1969, aged 79. About 250,000 people attended his funeral, and his body was embalmed and put on display, against his will, which asked that he be cremated and his ashes scattered across a united Vietnam. North Vietnam won the war in 1975.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When North Vietnamese forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> on 30 April 1975 their tanks carried the words "You are still marching with us, Uncle Hồ", and South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s former capital was renamed Ho Chi Minh City in his honour. His image hangs in public buildings and schoolrooms. William Duiker suggests that the cult of personality around him drew on traditions of authority in Vietnamese society, and that under Hồ Vietnamese Marxism developed as a kind of "reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>". In Vietnamese folk religion some identify him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jade-emperor">Jade Emperor</a>, a cult the government tolerates with unease; a movement founded in 2001 reportedly had around 24,000 followers by 2014.</p><p>Writers long argued whether he was fundamentally a nationalist or a communist; recent scholarship has shown his longstanding commitment to communist ideology, and official Vietnamese historiography rejects portrayals of him as merely a nationalist. His thought inspired leaders and movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>, and as a communist he was relatively well regarded in the West. Time magazine included him in 1998 among the 100 most important people of the twentieth century, and in 1987 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recommended that its members mark the centenary of his birth. Places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a> bear his name, and according to Vietnam's foreign ministry as many as 20 countries have monuments or statues to him.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Ho Chi Minh&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>William J. Duiker, The Communist Road to Power in Vietnam (Westview Press, 1982)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale<span>1871</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ho Chi Minh (1890–1969) was a Vietnamese communist revolutionary who spent three decades abroad, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party">Indochinese Communist Party</a> and led the Việt Minh to victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As president of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> he ruled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, carried out a land reform with many executions and directed the war for the South until his death.</em></p>

